Remove unused rwlock.hpp header and includes

rwlock.hpp was left over after migrating to atomic shared pointers
for branches configuration. No code references ReadGuard/WriteGuard
any longer, so drop the header and its stale includes.
state: atomic ref_count in OpenFile

ref_count is read and written from multiple FUSE worker threads
during open/release. Make it std::atomic<int> and provide a move
constructor so OpenFile remains movable when the underlying atomic
is non-movable.
branches: re-link default_minfreespace pointer on copy/move; atomically publish branch mode changes

Branches using the default minfreespace held a reference to their
owning Impl's _default_minfreespace. After a copy or move the
pointer still referenced the source Impl, which could become
invalid or diverge after further configuration changes. Switch to
a pointer and re-link it to the destination Impl on copy/move.

While here, convert set_branch_mode to do an atomic_store of a
newly constructed Impl rather than mutating the shared pointer
target in place. Readers holding an atomic_load snapshot are no
longer affected by a concurrent mode change.
FUSE: require valid file/dir handle; drop open_files fallback

Several FUSE ops fell back to looking up a FileInfo via
state.open_files when the kernel-provided fh was zero, returning
-ENOENT if the lookup failed. This path papered over handle-tracking
bugs and added cost on every call. Use the handle directly and
return -EBADF when it is invalid - the kernel should always hand us
a valid fh for an open resource.

Also simplify FileInfo::from_fh / DirInfo::from_fh and their to_fh
helpers: callers now handle the null-pointer case explicitly so the
ASSERT_NOT_NULL checks are redundant.

Covers fallocate, fchmod, fchown, fgetattr, flock, ftruncate,
futimens, read, release, releasedir, write, statx, ioctl (file +
dir), readdir (sequential, concurrent-on-read,
concurrent-on-search), and the passthrough fast paths in
create/open.
FUSE: pass Branches::Ptr by value with empty-branches guards

Previously most FUSE ops took a reference to the live cfg.branches
object. With atomic_load now producing a shared_ptr snapshot, pass
it by value: the op works against a single stable view of the
configuration for its whole lifetime, immune to concurrent
reconfiguration.

Also bail out with -ENOENT when the policy returns an empty branch
set rather than dereferencing branches[0]. This was reachable if a
policy rejected every branch (e.g. everything readonly while
looking for a writable target).

Covers access, chmod, chown, create, getattr, getxattr, ioctl,
link, listxattr, mkdir, mknod, open, readdir (cor/cosr),
readlink, removexattr, rename, rmdir, setxattr, statfs, statx,
symlink, truncate, unlink, utimens.

While here, in _getattr only dereference st_->st_mode when lstat
succeeded (it is otherwise uninitialized).
fuse_getattr/statx: populate timeouts in fake_root and controlfile replies

The fake-root and control-file fast paths returned a synthetic stat
without touching the timeout struct. Callers that took those paths
ended up sending whatever the timeout struct happened to hold,
which is uninitialized memory for the control-file path and
whatever a prior call left there for the fake-root path. Kernel
behavior is undefined when the timeout is garbage.

Pass timeout_ into both helpers and set entry/attr from the
configured cache values.
fuse_readdir: atomic initialized flag; bound error string; check msgbuf allocation

- ReadDir::_initialized is set by the first thread through the
  lazy-init path and read by every subsequent caller; make it
  std::atomic<bool> so the read/write pair has a happens-before
  relationship.

- The 'no valid mergerfs branch found' error directory entry used
  strcpy into a fixed-size dirent buffer. The message happens to
  fit today but the buffer is only NAME_MAX wide; switch to a
  length-bounded strncpy driven by the constexpr string size.

- The getdents implementations for seq/cor/cosr allocated a msgbuf
  without checking for allocation failure and then dereferenced it.
  Return -ENOMEM when the allocation fails.
fuse_readlink: always null-terminate buffer

Old code only null-terminated when the returned/copied length was
strictly less than the caller-supplied bufsize_, leaving the buffer
unterminated on an exact-fill. Since buf_ is consumed as a C string,
reserve one byte for the terminator up front and unconditionally
terminate.
fuse_statfs: scale with long double to avoid integer overflow

The branch-aggregation code computed
  blocks * f_frsize / min_frsize
as u64 arithmetic. With large filesystems the intermediate product
blocks * f_frsize overflows 64 bits long before the division can
bring the result back into range, producing garbage bavail/bfree
values reported to userspace.

Compute the scale factor once in long double and multiply each
count by it, so the only narrowing happens in the final cast back
to fsblkcnt_t.
fs_movefile_and_open: -ENOSPC when no destination branch available

The create policy can legitimately return success with an empty
destination list (e.g. every candidate rejected). Previously we
then dereferenced dst_branch[0] and faulted. Return -ENOSPC so the
original write that triggered the move propagates a sensible errno
to userspace.
str: null-safe same() and startswith()

Both helpers previously called strcmp/pointer-dereference directly
on their arguments, so a stray NULL (e.g. an unset env string or an
absent xattr) crashed the process. Guard both helpers: same()
returns true only if both pointers are equal, startswith() returns
false if either side is NULL.
log: default debug log path to empty; skip set when empty

libfuse previously initialized its debug log filepath to
"/dev/stderr", forcing the config layer to reopen stderr on every
load whether or not the user asked for it. Default the libfuse log
path to "" and have the mergerfs config layer only forward the
request to libfuse when the user supplied an explicit path.
libfuse: remove userdata from init/destroy APIs; drop unused fuse_reply_ioctl_iov

The low-level init/destroy callbacks were passed a userdata pointer
that mergerfs never consumed - the caller threaded it through
fuse_lowlevel_new only to hand it back unchanged. Remove the
parameter from the ops struct, from fuse_lowlevel_new(_common),
and from the internal fuse_ll struct; update fuse_lib_init and
fuse_lib_destroy accordingly.

Also drop fuse_reply_ioctl_iov: no caller in-tree invokes it and
the public API is defined by this vendored libfuse fork.
libfuse: harden fuse.cpp against NULL nodes; use lock guards; check allocations

Collapse get_node/get_node_nocheck into a single function that
returns NULL when the node is absent rather than abort()ing.
Callers that previously assumed non-NULL now check the return value
and either reply ESTALE/ENOMEM or fall through gracefully; a few
places where the node is load-bearing (e.g. unhash during remove)
use fatal::abort with a descriptive message.

Switch mutex_lock / mutex_unlock pairs to mutex_lockguard so early
returns on error paths no longer leak the lock, and add NULL-safety
around fuse_dh in readdir/readdir_plus, fuse_dirents_init error
returns, the getattr path when both name and fh are empty, and the
msgbuf allocation in fuse_lib_read.

Pull the new xstrdup/xmalloc wrappers from xalloc.hpp for the root
node name so the common-case OOM path terminates with a diagnostic
instead of a silent corrupted node.

Also guard fuse_session_destroy against a NULL session - called
from error-recovery paths.
libfuse: use atomic fuse_session pointer in signal handler

The signal handler reads the global session pointer from an async-
signal context while another thread installs/removes the session
via set/remove_signal_handlers. Use std::atomic<fuse_session*> with
acquire/release ordering so the handler either sees NULL or a fully
published session, never a torn pointer.
libfuse: thread_pool/objpool/fuse_loop - safer memory and iteration

- ThreadPool::destroy used to iterate _threads directly while another
  thread could be adjusting the same vector. Snapshot the vector
  under the mutex and iterate the local copy for pthread_cancel /
  pthread_join.

- ObjPool::construct now returns NULL when the pooled allocator
  returns NULL instead of invoking placement-new on a null
  pointer. In destroy, compute to_node(obj) before running the
  destructor - the destructor may scrub bytes the to_node helper
  reads.

- In the async/sync workers in fuse_loop, msgbuf_alloc can return
  NULL under memory pressure. Fail the worker cleanly with a
  diagnostic rather than dereferencing. Also introduce a small
  _nproc() helper that treats hardware_concurrency()==0 as 1, so
  the downstream min/max arithmetic doesn't produce a zero thread
  count.
libfuse: small safety and portability fixes across opt/cpu/fusermount/maintenance_thread

- fuse_opt: reformat process_opt_param to project style; return the
  rv from sscanf through a named local so the error branch prints
  the failing option cleanly.

- cpu.cpp: when parsing /sys/devices/system/cpu/*/topology/core_id,
  bail out cleanly on stream read failure rather than recording an
  uninitialized core_id.

- fusermount: free(copy) on the success path of parsing the mount
  option (previously leaked on every mount), and replace the fixed-
  buffer sprintf into procfile with snprintf bounded by the buffer
  size.

- maintenance_thread: widen the iteration count from int to u64
  so a long-lived daemon doesn't wrap after ~68 years of ticks
  (real effect: silence signed-overflow warnings under UBSan).
Formatting: normalize brace style and parameter alignment

No functional change: libfuse/helper.cpp (fuse_daemonize,
fuse_helper_opt_proc, fuse_parse_cmdline) and mergerfs
fuse_init.cpp (_want helpers) reformatted to match the project's
GNU-style brace placement and aligned parameter lists.
